.sh DIAGNOSTICS
Appendix I of the user's manual describes every error message produced directly by the system.
It is hoped that all Slogo's error messages should be completely self-explanatory.
Messages may also be produces by GPAC, the UNIX graphics package.
These are usually easy to understand.
As well as error messages, Slogo may produce messages of the form
	Assertion failed: <some gibberish>
	IOT trap -- core dumped
.br
may be produced.
If an assertion fails, there is good reason
to believe that you have found a bug.
Please send me mail (userid=td), telling me the circumstances, anything
the system may have printed prior to dumping core,
and where the dump may be found.
If Slogo has indigestion, it may print messages of the
form
	<Somebody> urped:(<number>,<number>,<number>)
.br
It will not exit after urping, since sometimes I want to go on and look for more symptoms.
You should probably type ctrl-\\ (ctrl-shift-l on the tek) and send
me mail.
Of course, Slogo can also produce error messages that make no sense
or dump core without warning.
.sh AUTHOR
Tom Duff.
.sh BUGS
Slogo is still, to some extent, under development.
A number of table sizes are not checked, so, in particular,
the system can blow its head off if you type a line that's too long.
If you get an error in the middle of drawing a picture, you can never draw
another picture.
There are other bugs (look in /mnt/td/bug/bugs if you're interested),
but most of the ones I know about were found reading the code, not running
programmes, and you're not likely to find most of them.
Send me mail if you want quick action on new bugs.
If its not urgent, use the bug command (if Slogo hasn't addled itself
so badly that nothing at all works).
